embedding: Embedded fields

A struct that embeds another gets the embedded type's fields and methods promoted: d.Name()
is d.Animal.Name(), and d.name is d.Animal.name. The emitter writes the path through the
embedded fields (one member access for each step, -> after an embedded pointer, which checks
for nil). Methods that an interface needs are also written as forwarding members of the
struct, so that the method tables of interfaces can find them.

A struct that embeds one C/C++ type has that type's members promoted too (mv.push_back(1)
for a struct that embeds std.vector[int]); with several embedded C/C++ types, name the
field.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#embedding
See also: gocpp explain methods, gocpp explain interfaces
Also known as: embed, embedded, promoted, promotion

Example (embedding.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    type Animal struct{ name string }

    func (a Animal) Name() string { return a.name }

    type Dog struct {
    	Animal
    	tricks int
    }

    func main() {
    	d := Dog{Animal{"rex"}, 2}
    	fmt.Println(d.Name(), d.name, d.tricks)
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    struct Animal;
    struct Dog;

    struct Animal {
        gp::string name;
        bool operator==(const Animal&) const = default;
        std::size_t gp_hash() const {
            return gp::hash_fields(name);
        }
        static constexpr auto gp_fields = gp::fields(gp::field(&Animal::name, "name", false, false));
        gp::string Name() const;
    };
    static_assert(sizeof(Animal) == 32 && alignof(Animal) == 8);

    struct Dog {
        gpkg_main::Animal Animal;
        int64_t tricks = 0;
        bool operator==(const Dog&) const = default;
        std::size_t gp_hash() const {
            return gp::hash_fields(Animal, tricks);
        }
        static constexpr auto gp_fields = gp::fields(gp::field(&Dog::Animal, "Animal", true, true), gp::field(&Dog::tricks, "tricks", false, false));
    };
    static_assert(sizeof(Dog) == 40 && alignof(Dog) == 8);

    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/embedding.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/embedding.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./embedding.gp:7: func (a Animal) Name() string { return a.name }
    gp::string Animal::Name() const {
        const Animal& a = *this;
        return a.name;
    }

    // ---- ./embedding.gp:14: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./embedding.gp:15: d := Dog{Animal{"rex"}, 2}
        Dog d{Animal{"rex"_gs}, 2};

    // ---- ./embedding.gp:16: fmt.Println(d.Name(), d.name, d.tricks)
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
    // gp[methods] col 14: value method Name: a plain member call; the body works on a copy of the
    //     receiver, or on a const reference when it cannot change it
    // gp[embedding] col 26: name is promoted from an embedded field: the C++ names the path through the
    //     embedded fields, one member access for each step (-> after a pointer, which checks for nil)
        gp::fmt::Println(d.Animal.Name(), d.Animal.name, d.tricks);

    // ---- ./embedding.gp:17: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
